# Copy to .env for a local run. Nothing here is a secret; the real values live in
# Modal Secrets (see modal_app.py).

# --- The kill switch (§3) ---------------------------------------------------
# Set to 0 to disable NEW analysis jobs and answer a clean "feature unavailable".
# Cached maps keep serving, because a cache hit costs nothing and never touches a
# recording. Read per request — flipping it needs no deploy.
CHORDS_ANALYSIS_ENABLED=1

# --- Auth (same Firebase project as Mo, §16.2) ------------------------------
# Locally, leave the Firebase keys unset and use the dev token instead.
CHORDS_DEV_TOKEN=dev-token
# FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=      # the key's JSON *content*, or base64 of it
# CHORDS_REQUIRE_AUTH=1               # production: crash rather than 401 everyone.
                                      # Refuses to start if CHORDS_DEV_TOKEN is set.

# --- Storage ----------------------------------------------------------------
CHORDS_DB_PATH=chords.sqlite3
# CHORDS_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://…  # set ⇒ Postgres; unset ⇒ SQLite above

# --- Quota (§16.4) ----------------------------------------------------------
# This service's OWN daily quota — an analysis and a Mo song are different
# spends. Cache hits never count against it.
CHORDS_DAILY_QUOTA=10

# --- Abuse hardening --------------------------------------------------------
# 0 disables a scope, which is the default so a local run is unaffected.
CHORDS_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=0
CHORDS_RATE_LIMIT_IP_PER_MIN=0

# --- Admin / takedown (§3) --------------------------------------------------
# Unset ⇒ the admin routes answer 503 and /healthz says "unconfigured".
CHORDS_ADMIN_TOKEN=local-admin-secret

# --- Analysis ---------------------------------------------------------------
CHORDS_MAX_VIDEO_SECONDS=600          # §18: songs, not DJ sets
CHORDS_JOB_DEADLINE_S=180
CHORDS_CONFIDENCE_FLOOR=0.5
# §20.4 — let a song's repeated sections vote their engine mistakes out. This is
# the ONE part of the theory layer that edits chords the engine actually
# reported, and neither `lint` nor `lint_sync` can see a bad edit (both check the
# song against itself). It is a flag rather than a constant so it can be turned
# off in production without a deploy. Measured on the real corpus it is worth
# about +0.003 delivered accuracy — real, but within noise on nine tracks.
CHORDS_THEORY_CONSENSUS=on
# MUST be a tmpfs / ephemeral mount. app/analysis/scratch.py refuses anything
# under $HOME, inside the working tree, or on an unrecognised filesystem.
CHORDS_SCRATCH_ROOT=/tmp/chords-scratch
# CHORDS_SCRATCH_ALLOW_PREFIX=        # ':'-separated extra prefixes, if you are
                                      # certain they are ephemeral

# --- Engines (§8 step 2 — chosen; see the benchmark table in README) --------
# These are the defaults in code; set them only to override.
#   chords: btc | chroma        beats: beat_this | librosa
# CHORDS_CHORD_ENGINE=btc
# CHORDS_BEAT_TRACKER=beat_this
#
# BTC is a git checkout, not a pip package (no PyPI release). The worker image
# clones it; locally, point this at your own clone of
# https://github.com/jayg996/BTC-ISMIR19 — it ships its own weights.
# CHORDS_BTC_ROOT=./vendor/BTC-ISMIR19

# --- Fetch ------------------------------------------------------------------
# YouTube answers datacentre IPs with a bot check — the most likely failure in
# production, and the one `ytdlp_source` logs at ERROR level so you can tell it
# from a video simply being private.
#
# **Cookies do not fix it.** That was measured, not assumed: across ten
# containers on ten distinct Modal egress IPs, three ordinary uploads resolved on
# two of them — 20%, and identical with and without cookies (6/30 in both arms).
# The check is per **IP reputation**, not per account. Retrying on a fresh
# container is worth more than any credential, because each one is a new IP; the
# real lever is a residential or rotating proxy.
#
# The settings below are therefore left unset here and in the deployed secret.
# They remain supported for age-restricted or members-only video, where a
# signed-in session genuinely is the mechanism:
#
#   ...COOKIES         a path. Local runs, or anywhere you can mount a file.
#   ...COOKIES_CONTENT the file's *contents*. This is the one Modal needs — it
#                      delivers secrets as env vars, and the worker mounts no
#                      Volume, so there is no file for a path to point at.
#
# Export a Netscape cookies.txt from a browser signed in to YouTube. The path
# wins if both are set. Treat either as a live credential for that account.
# CHORDS_YTDLP_COOKIES=/run/secrets/youtube-cookies.txt
# CHORDS_YTDLP_COOKIES_CONTENT=
# CHORDS_FFMPEG=ffmpeg
#
# **This is the setting that fixes the bot check.** A rotating residential pool,
# as `scheme://user:pass@host:port`. Both adjectives matter:
#
#   rotating     one address per request. A pool that pins you to a single exit
#                gets that exit flagged and then fails forever.
#   residential  the check is on address *reputation*. A static datacentre IP —
#                which is what a cloud provider's static-egress feature sells,
#                Modal's `modal.Proxy` included — is the fingerprint it looks
#                for, and is strictly worse than the unproxied default: it
#                trades a 1-in-6 draw for a permanent no with no retry into.
#
# The cost is bounded by the cache, which is what makes it affordable: a chord
# map is stored per videoId and shared across every user, so a proxied fetch is
# paid once per *song ever*, not once per play. At `-f worstaudio[abr>=64]` a
# four-minute song is ~2-4 MB, so at typical 2026 pricing ($2-8/GB) that is
# roughly a cent the first time anyone analyses a song and nothing after.
#
# Setting this also shrinks the worker's retry budget from twelve containers to
# three (`modal_app.egress_attempt_budget`) — unproxied the retry *is* the
# mitigation; proxied it is only insurance.
# CHORDS_YTDLP_PROXY=

# --- CORS -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Empty ⇒ no CORS middleware at all. The iOS app isn't a browser.
CHORDS_CORS_ORIGINS=
